## Econ: the economics of self-improvement: what an improvement costs, when it pays, and which to buy with a budget
## Import it with an alias of your choice: "ec:" u_se< "Econ".
## Put libs/Econ/src on XETAL_PATH ("just path"); the reference is libs/Econ/docs.
## Names with l: are exported; the others are private to this file.
##
## An improvement (a tool, a procedure, a better policy) costs an
## investment i once and lowers the cost of each later task of its
## kind from b (the baseline) to a. It pays only when it is used often
## enough: c tasks cost c * b without it and i + c * a with it. Costs
## are in any one unit (tokens, model calls); counts c may be arrays,
## so a whole workload, or a cumulative curve over r_ange n, is one
## call. This is the ski-rental problem: rent (b a task) or buy (i).

### Costs of a policy

## `(i, b, a, k) a_fter c`: the cost of c tasks of one kind under the
## rule "improve when the k-th task at the baseline is done": the first
## k tasks (or c, if fewer) cost b each, then i is paid once and the
## rest cost a. k = 0 improves at once (eager), a k larger than any c
## never improves (the fixed harness). Item by item over c, so over
## r_ange n it is the cumulative cost after each task. A negative k
## (what s_kiRental gives for an improvement that never pays) never
## improves.
## >> "ec:" u_se< "Econ"
## >> (3000, 1000, 450, 6) ec:a_fter 1 6 7 40
## 1000 6000 9450 24300
l:a_fter := { (i, b, a, k) c -> k < 0 ? c * b; (b * k m_in c) + (i * c > k) + a * 0 m_ax c - k }

## `(i, b, a) f_ixed c`: the cost of c tasks never improving, c * b.
## >> "ec:" u_se< "Econ"
## >> (3000, 1000, 450) ec:f_ixed 1 10
## 1000 10000
l:f_ixed := { (i, b, a) c -> c * b }

## `(i, b, a) e_ager c`: the cost of c tasks improving before the
## first, i + c * a (nothing when c is 0).
## >> "ec:" u_se< "Econ"
## >> (3000, 1000, 450) ec:e_ager 0 1 10
## 0 3450 7500
l:e_ager := { (i, b, a) c -> (i, b, a, 0) l:a_fter c }

## `(i, b, a) h_indsight c`: the least cost of c tasks, knowing c in
## advance: the cheaper of never improving and improving at once.
## No rule that does not know c can do better.
## >> "ec:" u_se< "Econ"
## >> (3000, 1000, 450) ec:h_indsight 1 5 6 40
## 1000 5000 5700 21000
l:h_indsight := { (i, b, a) c -> ((i, b, a) l:f_ixed c) m_in (i, b, a) l:e_ager c }

### When it pays

## `b_reakEven (i, b, a)`: the number of tasks at which improving at
## once and never improving cost the same, i / (b - a). Beyond it the
## improvement has paid for itself. When a >= b it never pays, and
## the value is -1.0.
## >> "ec:" u_se< "Econ"
## >> ec:b_reakEven (3000, 1000, 450)
## 5.454545454545454
l:b_reakEven := { (i, b, a) -> b <= a ? -1.0; i / b - a }

## `s_kiRental (i, b, a)`: the rule that waits: k, the break-even
## rounded up, so that "improve after k baseline tasks" never costs
## more than twice the hindsight cost, whatever c turns out to be; -1
## (never improve) when it never pays.
## >> "ec:" u_se< "Econ"
## >> ec:s_kiRental (3000, 1000, 450)
## 6
l:s_kiRental := { s -> c_eiling l:b_reakEven s }

## `(i, b, a, k) r_atio c`: how many times the hindsight cost the rule
## "improve after k" pays for c tasks (1 is the best possible).
## >> "ec:" u_se< "Econ"
## >> (3000, 1000, 450, 6) ec:r_atio 1 7 40
## 1.0 1.5365853658536586 1.1571428571428573
l:r_atio := { (i, b, a, k) c -> ((i, b, a, k) l:a_fter c) / (i, b, a) l:h_indsight c }

### A budget

## `m a_llocate (i, s, n)`: which improvements to buy with a budget m,
## given each one's investment i, its saving per use s (b - a) and its
## expected number of uses n (three vectors of one length): a mask,
## 1 for each one bought. Greedy by return on investment: the net
## value n * s - i over i, best first, only those that pay, bought
## while the running total of investments stays within m.
## >> "ec:" u_se< "Econ"
## >> 5000 ec:a_llocate (3000 1000 2000 4000, 500 100 400 50, 20 5 10 100)
## 1 0 1 0
l:a_llocate := { m (i, s, n) ->
  v := (n * s) - i
  o := r_ev g_rade v / i
  p := o s_elect v > 0
  t := p & m >= '+ s_\ p * o s_elect i
  (g_rade o) s_elect t
}

## `(i, s, n) n_et mask`: the net value of the improvements a mask
## buys: what they save over their expected uses, less what they cost.
## >> "ec:" u_se< "Econ"
## >> (3000 1000 2000 4000, 500 100 400 50, 20 5 10 100) ec:n_et 1 0 1 0
## 9000
l:n_et := { (i, s, n) mask -> '+ r_/ mask * (n * s) - i }
